# Recorded results and limits

The client edition carries the same saved scientific evidence as the source project. Exporting or passing CPU CI does not constitute a new GPU experiment.

## Original flow experiment

The native model combines five 320-byte packet representations, 20 sizes and 20 arrival intervals into 443 tokens. Four 256-wide Mamba blocks feed a six-class head. Compatible CICIoT2022 data contains 8,323 training, 1,040 validation and 1,041 test flows.

| Seed | Completed training | Test accuracy |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 120 epochs / 7,920 updates | 91.26% |
| 1 | 120 epochs / 7,920 updates | 84.05% |
| 2 | 120 epochs / 7,920 updates | 84.63% |

Mean accuracy is **86.65%**, with a **4.00 percentage-point sample standard deviation**. The paper reports 97.50% under different conditions. Our batch size 128 / maximum learning rate 0.001 follow the inspected source preset; the paper states 64 / 0.002. A separate 132-update masked-pretraining run tested functionality; it did not recreate the paper's full pretraining corpus. The main experiments used the released pretrained checkpoint.

Validation-fitted temperature scaling preserved every predicted class. Mean NLL changed from 0.4416 to 0.4172 and 15-bin calibration error from 7.03% to 3.82%. At the illustrative 0.90 acceptance threshold, seed 0 accepted 620 flows with 9 mistakes before scaling and 893 with 37 afterward. Better calibration does not guarantee safer alert acceptance.

## Both CSVs in the packet model

CICIDS2017 contains **1,410,255 rows**; UNSW contains **79,881**. Each has 1,500 payload columns, four metadata columns and a label. Every payload cell was validated. The native packet model consumes bytes only: metadata and labels are not model inputs. Its adaptation uses 378 tokens and a two-class head; its size/interval sequences are empty.

Payload hashes define one global train/validation/test split across both sources. Duplicate packets stay in one partition; conflicting labels remain disclosed as soft targets. Training used capped group subsets and replacement sampling. All six native arms completed 1,000 updates with one model seed; all nine simple controls completed.

| Training source / initialization | CIC held-out balanced accuracy | UNSW held-out balanced accuracy |
|---|---:|---:|
| CIC / pretrained | 98.67% | 53.73% |
| CIC / scratch | 50.00% | 50.00% |
| UNSW / pretrained | 71.52% | 93.22% |
| UNSW / scratch | 50.00% | 50.00% |
| Joint / pretrained | 97.56% | 94.29% |
| Joint / scratch | 55.57% | 50.00% |

These are group-weighted metrics. An UNSW metadata-only control reached **99.34%**, above the neural models on that test. All 25,930 held-out unlabeled classes matched joint-model evaluation. Two strict raw-score comparisons failed at the retained tolerance; class agreement does not establish identical logits.

## Evidence and remaining work

| Record | What it supports |
|---|---|
| [Flow results](docs/customer/evidence/results.json) | Three-seed training, evaluation, checkpoints and metric summaries |
| [Calibration results](docs/customer/evidence/calibration/results.json) | Paired confidence metrics and fixed-threshold tradeoffs |
| [Packet results](docs/customer/evidence/packet-study/results.json) | Six arms, two source tests, support and group/row metrics |
| [Packet inference comparison](docs/customer/evidence/packet-study/unlabeled/agreement.json) | Complete class agreement and retained numerical failures |
| [GB10 portability receipt](docs/portability-evidence.json) | Native build, numerical gates, optimizer/reload and inference checks |

The original flow data includes five exact train/validation overlaps and six train/test overlaps. Prior pretrained-data exposure is unknown. CSV payload grouping does not establish independent captures; the selected CIC test subset contains no PortScan rows and only four DDoS rows. Neither experiment validates customer traffic, unknown attacks or production alert thresholds.

Graph export failed in the tested custom-convolution path. Live capture, reliable flow assembly, sustained end-to-end throughput and NPU/SmartNIC compilation remain future work. A hardware deployment would need those components plus operator support, numerical parity and accuracy/latency tests on the target device.
